<h1>R-tree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
(1) A <a href="spatialAccessMethod.html"><em>spatial access method</em></a> that splits space with hierarchically nested, and possibly overlapping, boxes.  The tree is height-balanced. (2) A <a href="recursionTree.html"><em>recursion tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="btree.html"><em>B-tree</em></a>, <a href="ptree.html"><em>P-tree</em></a> (2), <a href="ptree.html"><em>P-tree</em></a> (3), <a href="rplustree.html"><em>R<sup>+</sup>-tree</em></a>, <a href="rstartree.html"><em>R<sup>*</sup>-tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
(1) Ondřej Pavlata's <a href="http://www.atalon.cz/r-tree/" target="_blank">Mg R-tree library(C++)</a>.
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
(1) <strong>A. Guttman</strong>, <em>R-trees: A Dynamic Index Structure for Spatial Searching</em>, Proc ACM SIGMOD International Conference on Management of Data, 47-57, June 1984. <br /> (2) Used in <a href="../terms.html#GCG92">[GCG92]</a>.  Suggested by Rama Maiti, 19 August 2001.
</p>

